#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171889 :question/id #uuid "f0111439-4fd8-5add-b95f-5e783178fd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9440" :question/problem "Draw a diagram that represents the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n at the points $(-2,1), (-1,-2),$ and $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "To sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,1) & =-2(-2)(1)\\,i+(-2)^{2}\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-2) & =-2(-1)(-2)\\,i+(-1)^{2}\\,j=-4\\,i+j \\\\ F(1,-1) & =-2(1)(-1)\\,i+{1}^{2}\\,j=2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/cf/cf51cba8548d1f342a9dd5c06b533782feb955fca4b90adfac5b6988d7e2e470.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412 :topic/difficulty 0.6937558215043725 :topic/id #uuid "46db30fb-cff1-5fef-85ad-641fbb5c68f2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087}] :topic/math-academy-id 3343 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :topic/title "Vector Fields"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699930 :question-generator/id #uuid "8fb7f282-aa08-5edc-80e5-3a335cc86d6f" :question-generator/script "generators/15/156b3c3c4454bd451da4486642643710f33e7fb67bc04b4e42174ea386843183/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a92a7327-4920-5e27-9447-1c7bf83